      "id": "4yy7fkh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Himalayan Mountain System"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Himalayan mountain system consists of young fold mountains formed by tectonic collision between the Indo-Australian and Eurasian plates.",
        "The Trans-Himalayas comprise the Karakoram, Ladakh, Zaskar, and Kailash ranges north of the Great Himalayas.",
        "Peninsular India features older relict and block mountains, including the Aravallis, Vindhyas, Satpuras, and the Ghats.",
        "Anamudi is the highest peak in Peninsular India, situated in the Western Ghats of Kerala.",
        "The Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats converge at the Nilgiri Hills."
      ],
      "id": "8cvb5es"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "87k1xfi",
      "content": "The Himalayas are the youngest fold mountains in the world. They extend for about 2400 km from Jammu &amp; Kashmir in the west to Arunachal Pradesh in the east. The Himalayas were formed due to the collision between the Indian Plate and the Eurasian Plate."
    },
