      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Trans-Himalayan Ranges"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "23k1y3b",
      "content": "• Karakoram Range – Contains K2, the second highest peak in the world."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uz6c26a",
      "content": "• Ladakh Range – Located north of the Great Himalayas."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats meet at the Nilgiri Hills in Peninsular India.",
      "id": "fmtwthm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8sz0wv3",
      "content": "• Zaskar Range – Located between Ladakh and Himachal."
    },
    {
      "id": "wnns3s2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Peninsular Mountain System"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mevgacz",
      "content": "The Peninsular mountains are older than the Himalayas. They are mainly formed of hard igneous and metamorphic rocks. These mountains are more stable and have experienced less tectonic activity."
    },
